{"numberOfHits":1,"results":[{"id":"GO:7770129","isObsolete":false,"name":"spermatid residual body","definition":{"text":"A membrane-bounded, anucleate mass of cytoplasm that is separated from one or more developing male germ cells during spermatogenesis and discarded. The residual body receives most of the cytoplasm and organelles not required by the spermatid or spermatozoon, such as ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, and excess cytoskeletal components, and is subsequently degraded, typically by phagocytosis by somatic cells of the gonad (e.g. Sertoli cells in vertebrates, gonadal sheath cells in nematodes) or by extrusion into the reproductive tract.","xrefs":[{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"12591244"},{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"3995554"},{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"7298721"}]},"comment":"The waste bag of Drosophila spermatogenesis, a cyst-level structure formed during sperm individualization that collects the discarded cytoplasm and organelles from the 64 spermatids of a syncytial cyst, is considered a narrow variant of this term.","synonyms":[{"name":"cytoplasmic residual body","type":"exact"},{"name":"residual body","type":"broad"},{"name":"spermatogenic residual body","type":"exact"},{"name":"waste bag","type":"narrow"}],"history":[{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"TERM","text":"spermatid residual body"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"waste bag"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"cytoplasmic residual body"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"residual body"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"spermatogenic residual body"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0110165 (cellular anatomical structure)"},{"timestamp":"2026-09-27","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A membrane-bounded, anucleate mass of cytoplasm that is separated from one or more developing male germ cells during spermatogenesis and discarded. The residual body receives most of the cytoplasm and organelles not required by the spermatid or spermatozoon, such as ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, and excess cytoskeletal components, and is subsequently degraded, typically by phagocytosis by somatic cells of the gonad (e.g. Sertoli cells in vertebrates, gonadal sheath cells in nematodes) or by extrusion into the reproductive tract."}],"aspect":"cellular_component","usage":"Unrestricted"}],"pageInfo":null}